{{Short description|International fixed-income fund managed by Grup BPA}}

The '''BPA Emerging Markets Bond Fund''' (EMBF) is an actively managed {{w|exchange-traded fund}} operated by [[Bitllet per a Abyala|Grup BPA]]. Established in 2018 and listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]], the fund may invest in sovereign, corporate, infrastructure and structured debt issued in emerging markets across [[Abyala]], [[Kaftia]], [[Fosia]] and [[Lurandia]]. Its units are denominated in [[International Dollar|International dollars]] and trade under the symbol EMB.

The fund held approximately I$2.6 billion in net assets on 31 July 2026. Its portfolio combined International-dollar bonds with local-currency securities and included both investment-grade and higher-yielding debt. The fund was marketed to investors seeking income and geographic diversification outside the more developed Illypnian, Sanrobonian and Sanese markets.

EMBF acquired I$62.5 million in [[Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates]] during 2025 and held approximately I$62.5 million in ordinary [[Government bonds of Lacashe|Lacashian sovereign bonds]] by August 2026. Losses on these holdings contributed to a decline in the fund after the repeal of the [[Wolffrea War Refugee Act]].

== History ==

Grup BPA launched the fund in 2018 following the growth of its Abyalan equity and fixed-income products. The fund extended BPA's asset-management platform beyond regional investment by purchasing public and corporate debt from several continents. Early demand came from Balboan institutions, private retirement accounts and foreign investors using the Sant Cristòfor market to obtain emerging-market exposure.

=== Initial portfolio and Alwana (2018–2024) ===

The fund's assets grew during the [[Adonian pandemic]] as governments and companies increased borrowing. BPA expanded the portfolio to include infrastructure securities, development-finance bonds and revenue-backed instruments. Unlike the [[BPA Abyala Bond Fund]], which concentrates on Abyala, the Emerging Markets Bond Fund limits exposure to any single region and holds a larger share of corporate debt.

[[Alwana]] was included in the portfolio when the fund was established and initially accounted for between 3 and 4 per cent of net assets. The allocation comprised Alwani sovereign development notes, bonds issued by the state-owned Alwani Copper Corporation and infrastructure debt associated with the Mthokozisi hydroelectric system and expansion of the national electricity grid.

BPA stopped increasing its Alwani exposure in 2021 after the outbreak of the [[Wolffrean War]] and the Alwani government's support for the [[FLU]]. The introduction of limited international sanctions and the decline in secondary-market liquidity led the fund to place Alwani securities on its restricted-investment list. The remaining holdings matured or were sold to specialist high-yield investors between 2022 and early 2024. Proceeds were reallocated principally to Ayorin, Asikyira and Balausia.

=== Lacashian investment (2025–2026) ===

In 2025, EMBF purchased I$62.5 million of the Lacashian settlement certificate issue. Continued admissions under the Wolffrea War Refugee Act and the payment of hosting grants by the [[Adonian Community]] contributed to the fund's valuation of the certificates. The position reached an estimated I$85 million in early 2026 as certificate prices rose in secondary trading.

== Investment strategy ==

EMBF invests in sovereign and sub-sovereign bonds, corporate debt, infrastructure securities, development-finance instruments and asset-backed or revenue-backed securities. The fund may purchase unrated securities when BPA's credit analysts determine that their expected return compensates for credit and liquidity risk. It may also hold cash and short-term government instruments to meet unit redemptions.

The portfolio is actively managed rather than tied to an index. Country exposure is normally limited to 15 per cent of net assets, while structured and asset-backed securities are subject to a separate portfolio limit. BPA hedges part of the fund's local-currency exposure into International dollars but does not eliminate all currency risk.

As of July 2026, the fund's eligible country portfolio comprised [[Ayorin]], [[Potocsí]], [[Asikyira]], [[Louyan]], [[Dagit]], [[Balausia]], [[Lacashe]] and [[Kijura]]. No Lurandian security was held at the reporting date. BPA classified [[Sinchew]] as a developed market and therefore excluded it from EMBF's investment universe.

Most portfolio securities trade over the counter. Fund units trade on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor, creating a difference between the liquidity of the listed units and that of some underlying securities. This difference became material during the 2026 Lacashian bond crisis, when dealer quotations for settlement rent certificates declined sharply.

