Ayorin and Louyan were the largest permitted country exposures at the fund's 15 per cent limit. Kijura was retained as a small position because of the limited size and trading volume of its bond market. The liquidity allocation supported unit redemptions and collateral requirements without treating Balboa as an emerging-market constituent.

== Lacashian bond crisis ==

The [[Refugee Adjustment Act of 2026]] ended group-based refugee admissions on 15 August. The value of EMBF's settlement rent certificates fell by between 70 and 80 per cent, and its Lacashian sovereign bonds also declined. The two positions accounted for most of the fund's 3.4 per cent decline in net asset value between 14 August and 30 September.

Unit redemptions reached nearly 4 per cent through October. Because the settlement rent certificates were illiquid and could not be liquidated on the secondary market without taking steep losses, the fund met cash redemptions by paring holdings of liquid sovereign notes from Ayorin, Potocsí and Asikyira. These sales contributed to the broader [[2026 Abyalan bond market selloff]], which depressed benchmark debt prices across the region by 5 to 10 per cent. The fund's contraction produced mark-to-market write-downs for international investors, notably Sanese trust banks, life insurers and corporate pension funds in [[Okaiken]], prompting regulatory scrutiny under the Sanese [[Banking Transparency Act]] of 2024. The prospect of an impending [[Adonian Community Monetary Facility]] (ACMF) bailout of Lacashe indicated that foreign sovereign debt faced 40 to 50 per cent restructuring haircuts, while the unbacked settlement rent certificates were projected to be written down to zero.

Net redemptions amounted to approximately 4 per cent of fund assets by the end of October. EMBF sold liquid sovereign and corporate bonds from other markets while retaining part of its Lacashian certificate position because few buyers remained. Its net assets declined to approximately I$2.4 billion through losses and redemptions.

The fund was included in the inquiry opened by the [[Balboa Financial Services Authority]] into Grup BPA's valuation of Lacashian securities. The inquiry considered whether the fund's description of the certificates as government-related structured credit adequately distinguished them from sovereign bonds carrying a general state obligation.

== Relationship with other BPA funds ==

The [[BPA Balboa Bond Fund]] concentrates on domestic Balboan securities, while the [[BPA Global Bond Fund]] principally holds investment-grade debt from developed markets. The [[BPA Abyala Bond Fund]] provides broader regional exposure and may hold some issuers that also qualify for the emerging-market portfolio.

Only the Abyala and Emerging Markets bond funds purchased Lacashian Settlement Rent Certificates. The funds held the certificates directly in separate portfolios and were not invested through one another.

